`default:` arm's\n     `lens[n++] = (uint8_t)sym`.\n   - `for (int n = 0; n < nlit + ndst;)` — `nlit + ndst` is int addition.\n\n4. `cp_block`:\n   - `int length = cp_read_bits(...) + cp_len_base[symbol];` — the addition is\n     `uint32_t + uint32_t` assigned to `int`. Check the Rust `.wrapping_add(...) as c_int`.\n   - `int backwards_distance = cp_read_bits(...) + cp_dist_base[distance_symbol];`\n     same question.\n   - `s->out - backwards_distance >= s->begin` and `s->out + length <= s->out_end`\n     pointer comparisons with a possibly negative `length`/`backwards_distance`.\n     Check the Rust `wrapping_offset(-(x as isize))` sign handling.\n   - `while (length--) *dst++ = *src++;` — confirm the Rust reproduces the\n     post-decrement semantics exactly, including `length == 0` (zero iterations)\n     and negative `length`.\n   - `memset(dst, *src, length)` when `length < 0` (C: `size_t` conversion → huge).\n     Report only; do not test.\n\n5. `cp_inflate`:\n   - `int first_bytes = (int)((((size_t)in + 3) & ~3) - (size_t)in);`\n   - `s->word_count = (in_bytes - first_bytes) / 4;` — C int division truncates\n     toward zero, so for a negative numerator it rounds UP. Check the Rust `/`.\n   - `int last_bytes = ((in_bytes - first_bytes) & 3);` — C `&` on a negative int\n     uses two's complement, giving 0..3. Check the Rust.\n   - `s->final_word |= ((uint8_t *)in)[...] << (i * 8);` — the shift operand is\n     an `int` (promoted uint8_t), so for `i == 3` and a byte >= 128 the C shift\n     `x << 24` overflows a signed int (UB) but produces the expected bit pattern;\n     the result is OR'd into a `uint32_t`. Check the Rust computes the same bits.\n   - `s->bits |= (uint64_t)(((uint8_t*)in)[i]) << (i * 8);` for the first_bytes\n     pre-load.\n   - the `do { ... } while (!bfinal)` loop, the `++count` placement, `free(s)` on\n     both exits, and that the `switch` has NO `default:` arm.\n   - `s->bits_left = in_bytes * 8;` — int multiplication, overflows for large\n     `in_bytes`. Check the Rust `wrapping_mul`.\n\n6. `convert_pix`: the `src++` per row, `src += bpp` per pixel placement relative\n   to the switch, and the fact that a `bpp` outside 1..4 writes nothing but still\n   advances `src`.\n\n7. The exported globals: verify the Rust `cp_fixed_table` const-fn construction\n   produces EXACTLY the 320 bytes of the C initialiser (count the C literal's\n   runs precisely: how many 8s, then 9s, then 7s, then 8s, then 5s), and that\n   every array's declared length and element type match the C.\n\nReport a concise table: item | verdict (MATCH / DIVERGENCE / UB-EQUIVALENT) |\ndetail. Only edit /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-TrEEjg/translation/src/lib.rs\nif you find a genuine divergence for a reachable, non-UB input, and describe the\nedit precisely."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1096,"cache_read_input_tokens":220211,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1096,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a545b5b-9a0e-47ab-b3c0-3ce9bc5f969e","uuid":"f1b8a7c4-5f91-434e-8628-23615a432a15","timestamp":"2026-09-05T07:33:43.902Z"}
