{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_anyp3ja7ftu6eofqeber6ucdtgjxbfa6ldkehljmuamay5ndmx5q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No edits were needed — I found no reachable, non‑UB divergence. Details below.\n\n## Verification table\n\n| # | Item | Verdict | Detail |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1a | `cp_build` L151 `(i << 4)` | MATCH | `i ∈ [0, sym_count)`, `sym_count ∈ {288, 32, 19, nlit≤288, ndst≤32}`, so `i ≤ 287` and `i<<4 ≤ 4592`. No signed overflow in C; `(i as u32) << 4` is bit-identical. |\n| 1b | `uint32_t slot` zero-extend vs Rust `slot as i32 as isize` sign-extend | MATCH (unreachable divergence) | `first[]` is built from `counts[]` whose entries sum to `sym_count`; `first[n] = Σcounts[0..n-1] ≤ sym_count`, and each `first[len]++` is bounded by the number of nonzero `lens[]`, so `slot ≤ 288` always. `slot as i32` therefore never goes negative and the address matches C's zero-extended index. `first[len]` can only exceed 2^31−1 if `first[]`/`counts[]` were themselves corrupted by the loop‑1 OOB write, in which case C is already UB (see 1c). |\n| 1c | assert relocation: Rust aborts in loop 1, C in loop 2 | UB-EQUIVALENT | Both loops iterate exactly `0..sym_count`, and `lens[i] ≥ 16 ⇒ lens[i] ≠ 0`, so the *set* of aborting indices is identical and the *first* such index is the same `m`. C reaches `assert(len < 16)` at `m` with the same expression text/line 148/function `cp_build`; Rust's `cp_assert_fail(\"len < 16\", \"cp_build\", 148)` emits the identical glibc string. Neither produces any library output first: everything written before the abort in C (`counts[]` OOB on the stack, `tree[slot]` and `s->lookup[]`) lands in the freed `calloc` state or the dead stack frame — never in `out` and never on a stream. `tree` is always `&s->lit/&s->dst/&s->len` with `slot ≤ sym_count`, so no pre-abort write escapes the state. **Caveat (unavoidable):** in C the loop‑1 OOB `counts[lens[n]]++` (up to `counts[255]`